(5356772, 770211, 30364, '<td valign="top">One of the best examples of a good ''bad'' photograph I''ve ever seen.  Surely even DPC''s leaden, lumpen ''technicals police'' will see the point of this glorious poke in the snoot of convention?  It''s a sublime example of using a camera to see the unseen, and to liberate the essence of the subject.  Apple, dandelion, spirit, exposure, frame ... I love it all.  10.  And the scoring kiss of death ... the Order of the Thumb:<br/><a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/image.php?IMAGE_ID=748778" rel="nofollow" target="_blank"><img alt="'' . substr(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/30000-34999/30364/120/748778.jpg'', strrpos(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/30000-34999/30364/120/748778.jpg'', ''/'') + 1) . ''" border="0" src="//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/30000-34999/30364/120/748778.jpg"/></a></td>', 'One of the best examples of a good ''bad'' photograph I''ve ever seen.  Surely even DPC''s leaden, lumpen ''technicals police'' will see the point of this glorious poke in the snoot of convention?  It''s a sublime example of using a camera to see the unseen, and to liberate the essence of the subject.  Apple, dandelion, spirit, exposure, frame ... I love it all.  10.  And the scoring kiss of death ... the Order of the Thumb:', '2009-03-15 00:00:54.000000', NULL, 1),
(5356773, 772733, 79141, '<td valign="top">good  shot</td>', 'good  shot', '2009-03-15 00:01:00.000000', NULL, 1),
(5356774, 772617, 101702, '<td valign="top">I''m hoping to leave comments on as many photo''s in this challenge as possible... out of (currently) 1116 images, I gave 29 of them a 9. Your image was one of those 29. <br/> <br/>I actually gave this an 8 originally, but when i came to comment, decided it was better than a lot of the 8''s I was commenting on. I can''t even begin to imagine how difficult it is to produce a macro shot of this quality, so well defind. He even has a little beard! Fantastic shot. <br/> <br/>Good luck for a strong finish in this challenge.</td>', 'I''m hoping to leave comments on as many photo''s in this challenge as possible... out of (currently) 1116 images, I gave 29 of them a 9. Your image was one of those 29.  I actually gave this an 8 originally, but when i came to comment, decided it was better than a lot of the 8''s I was commenting on. I can''t even begin to imagine how difficult it is to produce a macro shot of this quality, so well defind. He even has a little beard! Fantastic shot.  Good luck for a strong finish in this challenge.', '2009-03-15 00:01:01.000000', NULL, 1),
(5356775, 770183, 30364, '<td valign="top">Wonderful.  Photographing people of eccentric appearance is irresistible, but so many photographers, even quite famous ones, seem to seize the opportunity to make exploitative or patronizing images.  There''s no hint of the ''subject-as-a-curiosity'' here.  No freak show.  Instead it seems to be a moment when this fabulous lady and this photographer met for an instant on equal terms.  Not as performer and witness.  Not as freak and robber.  Just equals.  And that''s where the immense charm of this portrait comes from; from the rare lack of guile on the part of either party.  There''s no ''loser'' in this moment of engagement; just two winners.  10.  And, I''m afraid, the curse of the dreaded Order of the Thumb:<br/><a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/image.php?IMAGE_ID=748778" rel="nofollow" target="_blank"><img alt="'' . substr(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/30000-34999/30364/120/748778.jpg'', strrpos(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/30000-34999/30364/120/748778.jpg'', ''/'') + 1) . ''" border="0" src="//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/30000-34999/30364/120/748778.jpg"/></a></td>', 'Wonderful.  Photographing people of eccentric appearance is irresistible, but so many photographers, even quite famous ones, seem to seize the opportunity to make exploitative or patronizing images.  There''s no hint of the ''subject-as-a-curiosity'' here.  No freak show.  Instead it seems to be a moment when this fabulous lady and this photographer met for an instant on equal terms.  Not as performer and witness.  Not as freak and robber.  Just equals.  And that''s where the immense charm of this portrait comes from; from the rare lack of guile on the part of either party.  There''s no ''loser'' in this moment of engagement; just two winners.  10.  And, I''m afraid, the curse of the dreaded Order of the Thumb:', '2009-03-15 00:01:01.000000', NULL, 1),
